metricsWithDefs={"MarketCapitalization":"A company’s market cap represents its complete dollar market value based on its outstanding stock value.  It allows investors to understand the overall size of companies instead of purely its sales figures and asset value. Multiplying the total number of shares of the companies with the value of one share gives its market cap.","PERatio":"It stands for Price to Earnings Ratio and is calculated with the Price of a share in the numerator and the Earning per share in the denominator.  Analysts us this number for comparison of a company with other companies in its segment, or to study the historical performance of the company itself or comparing the company with the market as a whole.  A high PE Ratio means the shares of the company is overpriced relative to its earnings.","PEGRatio":"It stands for PE-Growth ratio and is an enhanced factor that takes the growth of the earnings into account to give a complete picture.  It has the PE Ratio as the numerator and the denominator has the company’s expected earnings growth.  A low PEG ratio is indicative of an undervalued stock.","Dividend per Share":"It is the sum of the total dividends issued by a company in a set period divided by the total number of shares issued by it.  It is a metric used by Analyst to evaluate the health of the company.  A company issuing more DPS is confident of its future economic growth.","Dividend Yield":"This is a financial ratio of the dividend issued by a company per share divided by the current price of that share.  Higher dividend yield might not always mean better investment opportunities but could also mean a declining stock price.","Earnings per Share":"It is a financial metric calculated by dividing the company’s net income by the total number of shares.  It explains how much a company makes for each value of its stock.  The higher the EPS the higher the profitability of the company and the investors will pay more for the company’s shares.","Revenue per Share":"This is a financial ratio of the total revenue earned by a company over a certain time period divided by the total number of shares. This metric is used by analysts to assess the productivity of a company over different time periods.","Profit Margin":"This is the amount of revenue a company gets to keep as profit after subtracting all its losses and is expressed a percentage.  If the profit margin is 20% it means the company has made a profit of 20 cents for every dollar in sales.  Analysts use this metric to assess the financial health and growth potential of a company.","Revenue":"It is the total money generated by a company in its normal business operations.  Revenue is only from sales. Various costs are subtracted from it to determine the net income.","Symbol":"Also known as Stock Ticker Symbol, it is an arrangement of characters (usually letters), that represent a security on an exchange.  As an example, stocks listed in NYSE can have four letters or fewer.  A company is the one that selects an available symbol while issuing shares to the public.  Investors use this symbol to make their trade.","AssetType":"In this stock analysis report, asset stands on the type of financial asset.  This is a Common stock.  It could also have been a Bonds and other types of securities.  These are assets that can be liquidated easily.","CIK":"A Central Index Key (CIK) number is a 10-digit number assigned by the US Securities and Exchange Commission (SEC) to track individuals, companies and other entities who have registered with them.  This is a public record and allows the public to track the filing history of the company","Exchange":"An exchange is a location, physical or electronic, where stocks and other financial instruments (such as bonds, commodities, derivatives etc.) are traded.  Most countries have their own exchange.  However the most prominent of these are New York Stock Exchange (NYSE), Nasdaq, London Stock Exchange (LSE) and Tokyo Stock Exchange (TSE).","Sector":"Investment sectors are categories formed that contain stocks and other investments sharing unique characteristics.  As an example, Financial sector includes all banking and insurance companies, Real Estate includes all residential and commercial developers, Industrial includes all manufacturing and machinery companies etc.  They are used to assess how well an economy is performing based on the performance of companies within the sector.","FiscalYearEnd":"Fiscal year end is last day of the company’s 12-month accounting period. This maybe different from the end of the calendar year based on the company’s needs.","EBITDA":"A fundamental metric and stands for Earnings Before Interest Taxes Depreciation and Amortization.  It is used to assess the profitability of the company.","BookValue":"Book value of a company is obtained by subtracting its total liabilities from its total assets.  This is used by accountants to value the total assets of the company.","ProfitMargin":"This is the amount of revenue a company gets to keep as profit after subtracting all its losses and is expressed a percentage.  If the profit margin is 20% it means the company has made a profit of 20 cents for every dollar in sales.  Analysts use this metric to assess the financial health and growth potential of a company.", "OperatingMarginTTM":"Operating Margin is obtained by dividing the operating income of the company with its sales revenue.  It represents how efficiently a company is able to generate profits.","ReturnOnAssetsTTM":"Return On Assets (ROA) of a company is a metric obtained by dividing its net income with its total assets.  This ratio is used by analysts to determine how efficiently a company utilizes its assets to generate profit.  A higher ROA indicates that a company can generate higher in come with lesser assets and is indicative of a more efficient company.","ReturnOnEquityTTM":"Return On Equity (ROE) of a company is a metric obtained by dividing its net income with its shareholder’s equity. This ratio is used by analysts to determine to determine the profitability of the company and how efficiently it generates them.  A higher ROE indicates that a company is better at converting its equity financing into profits.","GrossProfitTTM":"Also known as sales profit, it is calculated by subtracting the cost associated with producing and selling its goods and services from the total revenue.  Analysts use this metric to assess a company’s efficiency in using labor and supply.  It excludes fixed costs such as rent, insurance and administrative expenses.","DilutedEPSTTM":"Dilutive shares include securities that could be converted to common stock such as preferred stock, stock option and convertible bonds.  Calculating Diluted EPS includes these future shares along with outstanding shares which will result in a drop in Earnings Per Share.  This metric assesses the financial health of a company.", "QuarterlyEarningsGrowthYOY":"This metric measures the increase or decrease in a company’s earnings during the last quarter as compared to the same quarter from the previous year.  This is considered one of the most critical metric defining the performance of the company.  More than growth what is important for a company is to beat analyst’s predictions.","QuarterlyRevenueGrowthYOY":"This metric measures the increase or decrease in a company’s sales during the last quarter as compared to the same quarter from the previous year.  Analysts use this to review the growth of a company.  A negative value indicates a drop in sales but need not mean it to be a poor company.","AnalystTargetPrice":"It is the price at which analyst determines the fair value of a stock based on its historic earnings and future predictions. Raising the target price indicates the stock price are expected to increase and lowering it would mean an expected decrease.  When determining price, analysts use future supply and demand, technical levels and fundamentals.  It is an estimated value based on the company’s future performance.","AnalystRatingStrongBuy":"A strong buy rating is issued by analysts when a stock is expected to dramatically outperform the average market returns.  It is an expectation of a 30-50% growth in stock price in the next 12-18 months based on expectations of certain positive financial outcomes.","AnalystRatingBuy":"Also known as a “moderate buy”, it is the recommendation of an analyst based on the expectation that a stock is expected to slightly outperform the market. One analyst’s “strong buy” maybe another’s “moderate buy” but both are indicative of the stock outperforming the broader market.","AnalystRatingHold":"It is the recommendation of an analyst based on the expectation that a stock is expected to perform in line with the market or the same as other companies in the same category.","AnalystRatingSell":"Also known as a “moderate sell”, It is the recommendation of an analyst based on the expectation that a stock is expected to slightly underperform the market returns. One analyst’s “strong sell” maybe another’s “moderate sell” but both are indicative of the stock underperforming the broader market.","AnalystRatingStrongSell":"A strong sell rating is issued by analysts when a stock is expected to dramatically underperform the average market returns.  It is an expectation of a 30-50% fall in stock price in the next 12-18 months based on expectations of certain negative financial outcomes.", "TrailingPE":"Trailing Price to Earnings Ratio is the price of a share divided by the earnings of the company in the preceding 12 months. This metric is useful in comparing the share price of the company in different time periods and also comparing different companies in the same sector.  However, since it relies on past data it does not reflect current or future performance of the company.","ForwardPE":"Forward Price to Earnings Ratio is the current price of a share divided by the forecasted earnings of the company.  It is a key metric of a stock’s value.  However forecasted earnings are an estimation and may not be accurate.  This is why some analysts prefer using Trailing PE in stock analysis.","PriceToSalesRatioTTM":"This metric is calculated by dividing the market capitalization of the company with its sales in the previous 12 months (Trailing Twelve Months or TTM). Another way to arrive at this is dividing the stock price of the company with sales per share.  Analysts use this to compare companies in the same sector.  If this ratio is low then the company maybe deemed undervalued and if higher, then it may be considered overvalued.  A drawback of this metric is, it does not take the earnings of the company into account.","PriceToBookRatio":"This metric is calculated by dividing the stock price with the book value of the company per share.  The book value of a company is the difference between the total assets and the total liabilities.  When this ratio is below one it represents an undervalued stock.  Investors use this metric to identify such potential investments.","EVToRevenue":"It is obtained by dividing the Enterprise Value of the company with its Revenue. It is a valuation technique used during acquisition of a company.","EVToEBITDA":"It is a ratio obtained by dividing the Enterprise value (EV) of a company with its Earnings Before Interest, Taxes, Depreciation and Amortization.  This metric is used to determine the value of the company prior to acquisition. A higher value is expected in high growth companies and low values in industries with slow growth.","Beta":"This is a statistical metric that is used to assess the risk of a stock with respect to the market.  It provides an investor how much risk will a stock add to his portfolio.  The beta of S&P 500 is 1.0.  Any stock with a beta less than this is considered less risky.  Any stock with a beta greater than 1 is riskier but will also increase the chance of a higher return.","52WeekHigh":"This is the highest value the stock has traded at in a period of one year.  Investors use these numbers to decide whether to buy or sell a share any time.","52WeekLow":"This is the lowest value the stock has traded at in a period of one year.  Investors use these numbers to decide whether to buy or sell a share any time.","50DayMovingAverage":"It is the average of the closing price of a share over the last 50 days.  It changes everyday yet does not fluctuate with the daily change in price of the stock.  Graphs are plotted with this metric over time and analysts use it to compare the daily stock price to study fluctuations.  However, since it uses historical data it will not change quickly to factors that cause daily fluctuations.","200DayMovingAverage":"It is the average of the closing price of a share over the last 200 days.  This is a key metric and is plotted as a line to determine the trend in stock value.  It is plotted mostly along with the 50 day moving average and if the two are diverging it indicates an increasing market momentum.  However, if the two are converging it means a lack of market momentum and a possible bear market.","SharesOutstanding":"It is the total number of shares of a company held by all its shareholders which includes individual investors, institutional investors and the company’s officers and insiders.  This number fluctuates over time typically due to the company issuing more shares or exercising employee stock options.","DividendDate":"It is the date when the dividend is to be made payable to the shareholders by the company.  This is determined in advance by the company.","ExDividendDate":"It is the date at which the company decides which shareholders are eligible to receive a dividend.  Only shares purchased one full business day before the ex-dividend date are eligible to receive a dividend."}

@app.route("/")
def welcome_page():
    return render_template(
        "index.html",
        timeline_presets=get_timeline_presets(),
        crisis_library=get_crisis_library_rows(),
    )


def get_crisis_presets():
    return set(CRISIS_PRESET_LIBRARY.keys())


def is_crisis_timeline_preset(timeline_preset):
    return (timeline_preset or "").strip() in get_crisis_presets()


def get_crisis_library_rows():
    rows = []
    for key, meta in CRISIS_PRESET_LIBRARY.items():
        events = meta.get("events", [])
        end_value = meta.get("end", "")
        status = "Ongoing" if end_value == "present" else "Historical"
        end_label = pd.Timestamp.utcnow().date().isoformat() if end_value == "present" else end_value
        rows.append({
            "key": key,
            "window": f"{meta['start']} to {end_label}",
            "label": meta["label"],
            "status": status,
            "anchor_event": events[0][1] if events else "N/A",
            "event_count": len(events),
        })
    rows.sort(key=lambda item: item["window"])
    return rows


